# Example environment variables for the Talos frontend.
# VITE_API_URL points the UI to a GraphQL endpoint. When using the mock
# server included in this repo, set it to http://localhost:4001.
VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:4003

# ── Public tunnel (optional, local dev) ──────────────────────────────
# When NGROK_AUTHTOKEN is set, `make up` starts an ngrok sidecar
# (compose profile `public`) that publishes the controller on a public
# https origin, and the controller auto-discovers that origin for every
# externally-reachable URL it formats (GCP Pub/Sub push endpoints,
# Google watch webhooks, inbound webhooks, approval links). Get a token
# at https://dashboard.ngrok.com/get-started/your-authtoken.
#NGROK_AUTHTOKEN=
# Reserved domain (ngrok free tier includes one static domain, e.g.
# example-talos.ngrok-free.app). STRONGLY recommended: without it every
# stack restart mints a new URL and provider-side registrations go
# stale. Claim yours at https://dashboard.ngrok.com/domains.
#NGROK_STATIC_DOMAIN=
# Explicit override — wins over ngrok discovery (production origin or a
# hand-managed tunnel). Scheme + host only, no path.
#TALOS_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ── Worker-identity reaper (controller; default OFF) ─────────────────
# Deactivates a DEPARTED worker's signing key after a window of liveness
# silence, so a key that left the fleet stops being trusted without an
# operator running deactivate-worker-identity by hand. Unset or empty =
# OFF and nothing is ever deactivated. Ordered runbook:
# docs/security/operational-runbook.md § "Enabling the worker-identity
# reaper". Chart equivalent: controller.workerIdentityReaper.
#
# Do NOT enable in the same restart that first opens the liveness network
# path — a worker that pings ONCE is in the population forever, so a fleet
# where only some workers reach the endpoint gets the rest reaped.
#TALOS_WORKER_IDENTITY_REAP_ENABLED=1
# Hours of silence before a PARTICIPATING worker's key is deactivated.
# Default 24. THIS IS THE SECURITY PROPERTY, and it is the first of three
# additive terms (+300s ping-replay window, +300s sweep, +60s key refresh
# — ~24h+11m at defaults). Clamped [1, 87600].
#TALOS_WORKER_IDENTITY_REAP_HOURS=24
# OPT-IN second arm for rows that NEVER pinged (registered before the
# liveness protocol). Unset/0 = OFF. It keys on last_seen_at — written only
# at boot registration — so it cannot tell a departed pod from a healthy
# worker up a long time on an old build. Setting it asserts a fact the
# controller cannot check: confirm `talos_worker_liveness_participants`
# equals your worker count first.
#TALOS_WORKER_IDENTITY_REAP_PRE_PROTOCOL_HOURS=168

# ── Backup-restore drill escrow key ──────────────────────────────────
# NOT a compose variable — the drill is not a container (it creates and
# destroys containers, so it would need the root-owned docker socket).
# Set this in the shell where you run scripts/drills/schedule.sh; it is
# baked into the generated launchd plist. Without an escrow source the
# drill refuses to certify a restore — reading the key from the running
# system proves nothing about recovering when that system is gone.
#TALOS_DRILL_ESCROW_KEY_CMD='op read "op://Private/Talos KEK/password"'
# Alternative: a file whose first line is the key.
#TALOS_DRILL_ESCROW_KEY_FILE=/Volumes/escrow/talos-master.key
